more green juice okay hey everyone I'm  Abby sharp  welcome to Abby's kitchen so as my  channel has really evolved over the  years I've seen a huge uptick in my  plant-based followers so of course as a  result I've really made an effort to  make more recipes for you guys who are  looking for some more vegan and play at  base recipes also in my own life I just  have found that I've transitioned to  eating more plant-based foods just you  know for a variety reason I'll talk  about but also the fact that it's kind  of being a nice challenge for me to play  with ingredients that I didn't  necessarily grow up with eating in a  very kind of mean potatoes British  household so that's been really fun to  me  and you know I'm sure godus will will  will come at me for this but I think the  benefits of eating more plant-based  foods is hard to dispute  so one there's of course the  sustainability and the environmental  considerations in fact drawdown which is  a world-class research organization that  analyzes global climate solutions named  eating a plant-based diet number four in  their suggestions on improving climate  change I also think it's pretty clear  from the evidence that eating more  plant-based foods can help reduce the  risk of a wide range of chronic diseases  including colon cancer cardiovascular  disease type 2 diabetes and more finally  of course for those who are concerned  about animal cruelty eating fewer animal  products means less animals die whether  or not you can do that in a humane way  of course is up for debate also I want  to point out that despite what the  internet trolls may say dietitians like  myself are not controlled by the meat  dairy industries in fact the Academy of  Nutrition and Dietetics has come out and  said that a well-planned vegan or  vegetarian diet can be healthful  nutritionally adequate and may have  benefits that can help prevent or treat  chronic diseases so with all that said  why don't I just go vegan  well simply put any kind of restrictive  set of food rules is just not healthy  for me given my history of disordered  eating so I've told my or threat SIA  story in my book the my flow cookbook  but every single day now I'm getting DMS  and comments on my youtube channel and  emails from all of you telling me about  your relationship with food and your  struggles that you're having in this  kind of wellness culture that we're  currently living in especially when it  comes to social media and the pressures  around that so I really felt like I  wanted to take some time to tell my  orthorexia story here on my channel as  well  so when I was like 17 years old or so I  just graduated from high school and I  sought out the help of a homeopathic  practitioner to help me with some basic  kind of digestive issues so gas bloating  digestive kind of pain or discomfort all  the kinds of things that we're talking  and hearing about all the time on the  internet especially on YouTube and her  suggestion of course was to go on  basically like a sugar detox cutting out  all the sugars in my diet even the  natural stuff so I had always had like a  generally balanced healthy diet I never  really thought that much about food I  had never really dieted my my diet  consisted of like a very healthy balance  sometimes there were turkey burgers with  salad sometimes there were grilled  vegetables with a little steak or you  know pasta or slice of pizza  I never overdid it I never felt the need  to kind of binge I never obsessed over  what I was eating I just was eating  ultimately intuitively even though I  didn't have a name for it then I just  was eating what felt good to my body so  my diet went from being relatively  well-balanced to a handful of really  restrictive clean eating food so big big  salads without dressing and steamed  vegetables galore grilled chicken  breasts and other really lean proteins  plain no sauce sugar free jello or other  kind of diet e products like that and so  yeah I I what started out as a desire to  kind of kick a sugar habit very quickly  spiraled into an overwhelming obsession  and fear of all foods that I did not  consider clean or healthy and so this is  what orth Rexy is all about really  orthorexia is an obsession with clean  eating with with healthy eating and  unlike other eating disorders like  anorexia where the concern is really on  quantity of food with with orthorexia  it's all about quality or perceived  quality right it's it's all about kind  of eliminating those toxins or unclean  foods from your diet and even though it  wasn't about whey  los for me weight loss was never the  goal when I was eating such a such a  small selection of foods and and  low-calorie foods at that I actually  shed about 50 pounds on my already you  know pretty small natural frame and the  only thing more scary and [ __ ] up  about that than the fact that like I'm  really lucky to be alive right now  is the fact that I was constantly being  praised for my ultimately malnourished  body and my commitment to clean eating  honestly I can't tell you how many times  that people would you know pat me on the  back end because they saw what I was  eating and tell me how good I was being  and how bad they had been the night  before for eating a Snickers bar so  basically what I was being taught as a  young seventeen year old girl was that  my moral value was based on the food I  was choosing to eat and my emaciated  controlled body so anyways at the same  time I was really falling into this  anti-social cycle where my own fear of  not having control over what I was gonna  be able to eat and my embarrassment over  my disappearing body maybe not want to  go out with friends or make friends but  at the same time you know being isolated  and and eating by myself only enabled my  eating disorder and my mind assorted  behaviors and my depressive thoughts so  for those of you who have struggled with  disordered eating you know that it's a  lot easier for you to get away with it  when you're doing so in isolation and  nobody's standing over your shoulder  being like eat that and now is 15 years  ago  scarily today with the rise in  popularity of clean eating and wellness  diets a lot of those things that I've  been talking about so much here on the  abbeys kitchen channel one of these  disordered eating behaviors have become  synonymous with moral superiority and  economic privilege and social class in  other words it's become cool to eat  clean I mean you can't really even go  out for dinner anymore with friends  without somebody talking about their new  or detox it's like we're trying to  one-up each other on the moral value  scale by restricting harder or more  things and even if you don't have  friends in real life who participate  it's really easy to find your community  online to help perpetuate your  disordered beliefs and behaviors now I  want to take this back for a second just  to reiterate that I'm not suggesting  that veganism causes orthorexia I'm not  saying that I've talked about that  before that is not my intention here  however there is a fine line sometimes  between wanting to eat healthy however  you want to define that and orthorexia  which is why for me you know for the  longest time it took so long for me to  even recognize that there was an issue  here because in my mind I was just doing  what was best for me and for my health  and from all the comments that I've seen  this concept is so pervasive I'm seeing  this in so many of the experiences that  you all have been sharing with me that  is the wellness diet promise but if  you're curious where your intentions lie  it really comes down to being inflexible  if the thought of going out to dinner  without knowing you know what's gonna be  served or how its prepared or not being  able to control the menu your way sends  you into a panic mode you may have a  problem if your diet starting to  interfere with your emotional  psychological and social well-being you  might have an issue you might be in the  best king shape of your life and still  in a health crisis you honestly cannot  judge a book by its cover this is why  even though I see environmental animal  welfare and health base reasons to go  plant-based I cannot and will not ever  be able to follow a strict vegan diet  same would go for things like a keto  diet or paleo we're in a train fasting  or whatever even though it may be well  intentioned and grounded in wellness I  know that having to follow a strict set  of rules would trigger my or x'q  tendencies and lead me back down that  dangerous road that I worked so hard to  get out of I also get that a lot of you  who practice a vegan diet will claim  that you know this is not  about our help it's about the health and  well-being of the animals and I totally  understand that I get that however this  might just be a difference in values but  I feel that I need to value and  prioritize my own health and well-being  over that of the animals in this case  some of you may not need to make that  choice and that's great but just based  on my own personality and history I know  that I do and I have to prioritize my  own well-being  so let's talk about my diet how I eat  and what works for me now I know that  people like rules they like categories  they like black and white  I mean Society has taught us that we  need these external regulators to help  us make decisions about our health and  what to eat but I have spent the better  part of a decade trying to learn how to  become comfortable with the gray and get  back in tune with my body through  intuitive eating so what tends to work  well for me is just eating foods that  feel good to my body make me feel  satisfied without the need for explicit  labels I mean you could technically call  me kind of like a flexitarian but I even  reject that because I'm honestly just  eating in a way that feels good to me in  that moment meal or day so usually that  means eating a lot of plants with some  dairy fish poultry or meat in modest  portions I tend to feel most satisfied  with some animal proteins and end  products in my diet but more importantly  I think just not having those strict  rules means I don't feel like repressed  or restrained in a way that makes me  just emotionally want to bounce between  two extremes which I know would be my  tendency if I impose these really strict  rules honestly if I went strictly vegan  I would be concerned that I would very  quickly become unbeaten and be eating  raw duck breasts in the back of a cab  like this guy no but seriously I could  never do that but I do know that it's  just not emotionally healthy for me I  also believe that a lot of the benefits  for the environment animal welfare and  for our health can actually be achieved  simply by consuming more plant-based  products and consuming animal products  in more modest ways so for example if we  just thought of meat more like a side  dish rather than always being the main  event or more kind of like a garnish for  example on a dish to complement beans  and legumes and pulses we probably  wouldn't have to rely so heavily on  factory farming we would be able to  enjoy the antioxidants and fiber found  in plant-based foods and we could still  participate in some of the social and  all activities that involve animal based  products I'm not here to preach to  anyone to change their diet I mean you  do what works best for you only you are  the expert in your own body but I really  just want to reiterate that I want  Abbey's kitchen to be a respectful place  for a variety of diets and for everyone  to eat in ways that feels intuitive and  healthy for them so if my story rings  bell for you and you feel like you may  be struggling with your relationship  with food into sort of eating we're  gonna leave some really great resources  in the description so definitely check  that out I would also love to hear any  kind of resources or strategies things  that have helped you along in your  journey to intuitive eating and having a  better relationship with food please a  reminder to everybody let's be  respectful on this video in the comments  section I do not want any diet hate  going on so let's keep it clean but of  course if you like this video be sure to  give it a thumbs up leave me a comment  below like I mentioned subscribe to the  channel and I'll see you next time on  Abbey's kitchen bye  [Music]
 

